r is valuable for  most people because  you're not you when you're hungry but  when it comes to weight loss  specifically  satiety is key it's really hard to  create any kind of caloric deficit  if you feel like eating your own arm off  but  each of these three food components have  scientific reasoning as to  why they support keeping you feeling  satisfied for longer  so for instance protein is the  macronutrient that requires  the most work to digest meaning its  transit time in your gi tract  is longer than that of other foods  protein has also been proven to reduce  ghrelin levels  which if you remember from some of my  earlier videos is the hunger  stimulating hormone one study found that  participants who consumed  30 percent of calories from protein  compared to those who consumed 15  of calories from protein consumed almost  500 calories a day less  when given free rain to eat as they  please  as for fat fat is the strongest  inhibitor of gastric  emptying also staying in the stomach for  longer  and therefore keeping you feeling fuller  even longer  and finally fiber helps to reduce the  glycemic impact  of carbohydrate-containing foods by  slowing down transit time  delaying stomach emptying preventing  blood sugar crashes  and reducing hunger so yes to fiber  protein and healthy fats in some  combination  in most meals and most snacks now tip  three  is to dress up those naked carbs  we've discussed limiting naked carbs as  a strategy for folks with pcos in my  video right here  but i actually think that this is a  really valuable mindset for  everyone to get on board with naked  carbs are foods  like white bread desserts white rice etc  in other words their foods lacking the  hunger crushing combination  so this is really just another way of  conceptualizing the hunger crushing  combination  as everyone will respond to different  imagery  and strategy but my suggestion is to  combine your naked carb  with some protein and or fat to help  boost the satiety factor  and slow the blood sugar and insulin  response  for example if you like white bread have  your white bread  i'm not gonna force you to give it up or  switch to a fermented sprouted bread if  that is not enjoyable to you  but smear that bread with some natural  nut butter and top it off with berries  or throw on some mashed avocado and an  egg this is not only going to help to  stave off hunger a lot longer  thereby reducing the likelihood of a  snack attack  but it will also keep your insulin  levels from jolting up and down  now tip four is to slow down  and tune in so i know i know i need to  take my own advice as i admit  that since becoming a parent i've become  the fastest  eater of life  it's not good for my digestion my  enjoyment of food  and if i was trying to actively lose  weight which i'm not  it wouldn't be good for that either one  review of the literature  did find that eating slower was  associated with eating less  while other research suggests that fast  eaters are more likely to gain weight  compared to  slower eaters this is most likely  because it takes about 20 minutes or so  for your stomach to communicate to your  brain  that you're satisfied and i don't know  about you but  i do not need 20 minutes to polish off a  pint of  ben and jerry's so my suggestion is to  carve out time as much as you can for  you to sit down  and enjoy your meal if that means you  need to pencil meal times into your day  planner  or set a google calendar alert so that  they're not just like an afterthought  do it now i want you to imagine your  hunger  like a gas gauge ask yourself where are  you  on the hunger scale from 0 to 10  before you start eating as you eat tune  into your hunger and ask yourself  again where are you now if you started  out of two or three and maybe you're  halfway through what's on your plate  are you a five now are you a six are you  still a three  it's okay not to see an abrupt shift or  even be able to recognize what it feels  like  to satisfy your hunger yet this is  something that definitely takes  a lot of practice to hear especially if  you spent a lot of time  really heavily restricting but now keep  eating  until you feel at a place that is  comfortable for you for some people that  might be a six or seven or an  eight especially for a lot of  experienced intuitive eaters  but for others it might feel really  uncomfortable to be  anything less than a ten that's okay too  i don't want to turn this into the  hunger fullness diet  but you might find that with enough data  collection  and enough mindfulness that maybe a 10  no longer feels  so good anymore you're never going to  know though of course if you just  rush through the meal and miss those  early warning signs that you're full  so that brings me to tip number five get  rid of eating distractions  even if you're eating at a snail's pace  it's hard to tune  into your body's internal cues if you're  surrounded by  external distractions i'm talking about  your phone your computer  tv driving walking doing all the things  at once as i admittedly have attempted  hashtag parenthood but we do know that  distracted eating is often one of the  biggest triggers for mindless eating  an analysis of the research found that  folks who ate with distractions  ate 10 percent more than those who ate  without distractions  it not only seems to have an impact on  how much you eat during the distracted  meal  but also in subsequent meals as well  as we often forget that we even ate or  how much we ate  it's like my hubby who will tell me that  he didn't eat anything all day  but then i've watched him eat a bagel  banana apple cheese and like half a  rotisserie chicken  while taking business calls over the  kitchen sink when we eat on autopilot  the food just kind of somehow makes it  into our mouths  so power down and invest in the time to  eat  uninterrupted and then use that mental  bandwidth  to refer back to that hunger scale from  within okay  folks on to tip six meal  prep because we're not great at making  mindful decisions when we're  ill-prepared  if we go back to that analogy of our  body as a car  and her hunger being the gas gauge it's  really hard to find the best deal on gas  if we haven't mapped out our route  when we get desperate we're likely to  overspend at  the bougie full-service station  the same goes for food and nutrition i  know we're all busy  but i do see meal prep as a very small  investment  so everyone does meal prep differently  some people like to pick  a few recipes and then make a big batch  of like a few full meals  to be divvied up into containers for the  week  personally i get bored of this i also  find it makes it really hard to honor my  cravings in the moment  because i feel obligated to eat like  five days of the same recipe  that maybe i was craving a whole week  ago  so i prefer to make meal components  and then combine them in different ways  throughout the week  so for example i might grill up a bunch  of chicken breasts and bake off some  tofu  make a big pot of quinoa or rice  and then chop up and roast a ton of veg  then i have the building blocks for  different salads  soups stir fries and sandwiches that i  can mix and match with you know like a  different sauce or garnish  so next is tip seven do a fridge and  pantry  makeover no i'm not actually saying that  you need to go  on a kitchen detox and throw all the  junk  before your diet begins i actually find  that in a lot of cases  this tends to trigger a last supper  binge  where you know you're thinking oh my god  i gotta eat all the chips and all the  ice cream in the house today  because diet starts tomorrow i'm  actually suggesting  that you simply reorganize things to  make  more nutritious foods more convenient  and more accessible  for me this is kind of an extension of  the meal prep tip  but it doesn't necessarily require more  cooking which is great  i always recommend washing and prepping  lots of nutritious stuff  and then placing it front and center in  the fridge or  pantry so that the moment hunger hits  it's the most  intuitive and convenient choice to make  if you really want a little chips or  some ice cream  it's okay to keep them in the house but  place them somewhere  where you'd have to actively seek them  out  not just constantly grab them because  they're the first thing that you see  again this encourages us to be more  mindful  and deliberate about our food choices  even more indulgent ones  which will in turn usually lead to  greater satisfaction  and less mindless overeating all right  tip number eight  approach trigger foods wisely if you've  made it this far you know that i'm not a  fan of  overt restriction and that means that i  don't think that any foods  should be completely off limits even  when you're trying to lose weight  if i was coaching on intuitive eating  and improving somebody's relationship  with food  or disordered eating recovery i would  suggest  allowing yourself said trigger food  every day and this may work for a lot of  people  who are trying to lose weight who have a  history of yo-yo dieting  so to explain this i'll share my story  when i was struggling with food my  trigger was  sugary cereal i was obsessed so as part  of my healing process  i started to just make it part of my  routine  i challenged myself to have a bowl of  sugary cereal  every morning without guilt or shame  until eventually the novelty kind of  wore off  now i have no interest in sugary cereal  when  it's in the house in fact i sign up for  a nostalgic cereal of the month delivery  club  and i have only had like a handful of  each box  having said that i also understand that  not everyone is at a point in their  relationship with food  where they feel confident having all of  their trigger foods just  hanging around so in this case  particularly in the context of  weight loss i would suggest either  mindfully  and intentionally planning to buy them  or go out for them and when you do  enjoying them  guilt-free my friend abby langer author  of the new book  good food bad diet calls this awareness  not restriction so yeah to me it's like  you're aware that this food is easy for  you to overeat  so you're being intentional and mindful  as you enjoy it  but you're still approaching it with  permission so it's not to trigger a  binge restrict cycle all right moving on  to tip nine  make water your bff so i think it's a  really  common piece of diet advice to not drink  your calories when trying to lose weight  which often follows that you should  avoid all juice  soda alcohol and sugary coffee drinks my  approach is to choose water for  hydration purposes  and to work everything else into your  diet  the same way that you would like a fun  ice cream cone with friends or  family friday pizza night the reason why  i'm more intentional about limiting  calorie-laden beverages compared to food  is that  we know it's easier to override our  natural hunger and fullness cues  when we drink our calories compared to  eating them  there are a few potential reasons for  this but experts believe that  cognitively  people have a harder time deriving  psychological satisfaction from drinks  because they just mindlessly chug them  back while we interact longer with food  through  smelling and biting and chewing and  intentionally swallowing  liquids also travel faster through the  gi tract so  they alter satiety and hunger hormones  like ghrelin  differently than food especially if it's  a drink with  little to no protein fiber or fat  research suggests that even when eating  intuitively  we just don't fully compensate for the  calories that we consume in liquids by  eating less food and as a result it's  easy to gain weight when drinking a lot  of calories in fact  one study on over 50 000 women found  that those who increased their intake of  sugar sweetened drinks from  one a week to one per day added 358  calories daily  and gain significant weight while women  who reduce their intake  cut 319 calories now if juice is your  thing  or you need like double cream and double  sugar in your coffee to function  in life then of course work that into  your day  as you would anything else fun that will  make your eating pattern  more sustainable and enjoyable i'm also  not going to get into the hierarchy  of healthfulness of beverages i'm well  aware that juice contains  more micronutrients than pop but what  i'm saying  is to choose water for hydration whole  foods for nutrients  fiber and satiety and sugar sweetened  beverages for  pleasure flavor and fun on to tip 10  soul search to find your beloved food  faves when you've been dieting  restrictively for a really long time  it's not uncommon for any  stereotypically  demonized or bad foods to become  overwhelmingly alluring you know you're  on the paleo diet and told you can't  have dairy  and even though maybe you never cared  much for ice cream before  you suddenly find yourself binging on it  and just can't stop  so take some time to figure out what you  actually  truly like this goes for so-called  healthy foods as well as the alleged  junk  through this you'll maybe discover what  special foods you'll enjoy  fully and more mindfully that will  result in feeling truly satisfied  rather than continually going on a hunt  for what you  really want and that also means diving  deep  to determine what diet alternatives are  sufficient alternatives  and which are not if you love halo top  great  but if eating a pint of halo top  triggers a satisfaction hunt for the  real deal  and in the process you need to eat a bag  of chocolate chips  a few low-fat popsicles and then plow  through your ben  jerry's probably not a great choice so  figure out  what foods you truly want and enjoy  and work them strategically into your  week all right so on to tip 11  focus on stress and sleep  now this tip deserves a whole video in  itself and also i know i'm a hypocrite  i've got the worst  sleep and stress patterns but i'll try  to keep this brief  long story short there is solid evidence  that  skimping out on these basic acts of  self-care  can be detrimental to weight loss  efforts evidence suggests that  lack of sleep can increase our hunger  hormone ghrelin  reduce our satiety hormone leptin and as  a result  can increase appetite and potentially  our weight  it may also interfere with the reward  center in the brain  resulting in greater cravings for higher  sugar  and higher fat foods it's therefore not  surprising that  a six-year prospective study found that  those who slept less than five  hours or more than nine hours a night  we're 35 percent  and 25 percent more likely to experience  a  five kilogram weight gain over six years  respectively  likewise we have ample evidence to  suggest  that increases in the stress hormone  cortisol  is associated with weight gain over time  this  increase in cortisol tends to result in  us gravitating towards  more accessible food because you know we  likely lack  the mental energy to plan and prep and  cook not to mention  we use food as a coping mechanism for  said stress  but one study also found that women who  reported  one or more stressors within a 24-hour  period  burned over a hundred calories less than  non-stressed women  so there is apparently some metabolic  alterations happening as well which is  really interesting  all that said i know from my own  experience with insomnia  and anxiety that this is all easier  said than done and that the mere thought  of  not sleeping or being stressed causing  weight gain  that can be enough to actually  perpetuate more stress and insomnia  so it can become a vicious cycle and i  would need a whole video  to help you unpack solutions for this  but i bring it up to give you some  hopeful encouragement to prioritize your  mental health  because so much of weight loss is not  just about choosing  kale over cookies and that brings me to  tip number 12  acknowledge your emotional eating  patterns and intercept  again this is a big big topic so i'm  really just going to scratch the surface  here  but in the words of my partner in crime  abby langer  try not to weaponize food it's normal  and healthy to eat in response to  emotions sometimes since  food is intrinsically and socially  linked  to celebration mourning culture and more  but when it becomes our only coping  mechanism to deal with our emotions  it becomes a dangerous weapon so try to  experiment with other coping strategies  for difficult times like  journaling walking hot shower  doodling meditation or talking to a  friend  and if you're finding yourself  consistently turning to food to manage  your emotions  a licensed therapist can definitely help  all right moving on to tip 13  choose whole foods more often  i don't believe in setting arbitrary  guidelines or rules on  how much of your diet should be from  whole unprocessed or mentally processed  foods  like yogurt or frozen fruit versus ultra  processed  snack foods or fast foods there is no  quality evidence that i'm aware of that  would inform a  hard and fast ratio like this and i  really just see it as another rule to  obsess over  and inevitably break so  my advice instead is to think of  convenience foods as  assistance to the whole foods doing the  heavy lifting in your diet  if that store-bought ranch dressing is  going to help you get a salad on your  plate  eat the damn ranch if popping a frozen  pizza into the oven once a week means  that you help reduce your stress  and get to bed at a reasonable hour  pizza party it is  pizza party focus on the majority of  your diet coming from whole  or minimally processed foods and let go  of any guilt associated with the rest  next is tip 14 move your body in ways  that feels  good i actually think it's helpful to  decouple  our association of exercise with weight  loss  yes of course exercise contributes to a  caloric deficit  which in turn can enable weight loss but  research suggests that  exercise doesn't contribute as much as  we might like to think to weight loss  efforts  and putting too much stock into it can  likely be detrimental  by giving people a false sense of  entitlement to extra calories because  they put in the work having said that we  know there are  tons of benefits to exercise  even if weight loss isn't one of the  most significant ones  so my suggestion then is to just focus  on  movement you enjoy you don't have to  fill a bucket of sweat  for it to count ultimately if you don't  enjoy the movement you're not going to  stick to it  or you're going to let it encourage  overconsumption as a reward  for a punishing regimen so incorporate  some daily movement into your day as an  act  of self-care ideally something to  support metabolic muscle like some  resistance training  but ultimately make it something that  you can stick to  for life and finally tip number 15  be kind to yourself think of this  journey as  a marathon not a sprint please  do not make it a sprint because if your  running skills are anything like mine  there's a good chance you're gonna fall  on your face and never want to  run again in other words if you rush it  statistically  you're way more likely to gain that  weight right back  and since we need to look at this as a  long game  i also don't recommend weighing yourself  too often and for some  maybe not at all the scale is just one  tool that may be supportive for some  people  and toxic for others so everyone will  have success markers that work best for  them  but if the weight loss stalls please do  not freak out  you're not failing so do not give up and  forego  any progress that you've made in the  healthy habits that you formed  shift the focus to other markers of  success at least temporarily  to help you maintain momentum even if  the number on the scale isn't what you  want to see  weight loss is so complicated and as  much as we want to simplify it to  calories in and calories out  our bodies often have other plans so the  best thing that we can do  is to work with our body not against it  and give ourselves some grace as you can  see my goal with these gentle nutrition  and lifestyle suggestions  is to shift the focus away from denial  and restriction  and toward more additive considerations  that focus on  self-care nourishment and optimizing  nutrition  a reminder that this is not an  exhaustive set of nutrition tips  and really if you're looking to find  solutions that work for you  you'll want to work with a dietitian for  personalized care  but if you're sick of the extreme diet  roller coaster and associated weight  cycling  i hope this video offered some  evidence-based long-term  lifestyle and behavior changes that you  can consider and that is all that i have  for you guys today folks  thank you again to wild for sponsoring  this video if you liked it be sure to  give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below on what you'd  like to see me review next  subscribe to the channel and i will see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye  you
 

